gsndesign:src-0015
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.1.4 Table 1:2-1 provides the definition and rendering of these elements."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:2.1.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "17" ;
    gsndesign:page "17" .

gsndesign:src-0016
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1.2  Core GSN
1.3  Argument Pattern Extension
1.4  Modular Extension
1.5  Confidence Argument Extension
1.6  Dialectic Extension"""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "6" ;
    gsndesign:page "6" .

gsndesign:src-0017
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:2.2 An assurance case can be defined as: A reasoned and compelling argument, supported by a body of evidence, that a system, service or organisation will operate as intended for a defined application in a defined environment."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "10" ;
    gsndesign:page "10" .

gsndesign:src-0018
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Assurance Case
Arguments and evidence intended to demonstrate that a system meets its assurance requirements."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0019
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:3.1 In the sense used in assurance cases, an argument is defined as a connected series of claims intended to establish an overall claim."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:3.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "10" ;
    gsndesign:page "10" .

gsndesign:src-0020
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Argument
A body of information presented with the intention to establish one or more claims through the presentation of related supporting claims, evidence and contextual information."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0021
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Claim
A proposition being asserted by the author that is a true or false statement."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0022
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Structured argument
A particular kind of argument where the relationships between the asserted claims, and from the evidence to the claims, are explicitly represented."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0023
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Footnote 3: ‘dialectic’ is defined by the oxford English dictionary as “Logic, reasoning; critical investigation of truth through reasoned argument, often spec. by means of dialogue or discussion.”"@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "11" ;
    gsndesign:page "11" .

gsndesign:src-0024
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Dialectic
The process of investigating truth. This can occur in a minimal form by simply challenging statements made in an assurance case, but can also take a graphical form within a GSN argument"""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0025
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """0.4.1 (...) The relationships represented in GSN are:
• The premise-conclusion relationship between supporting goals and their parent goal;
• The support that solutions provide for goals;"""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "11" ;
    gsndesign:page "11" .

gsndesign:src-0026
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:4.2 The purpose of GSN is to document how claims (conclusions, represented in GSN as goals) are said to be supported by sub-claims (premises, also represented in GSN as goals)."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "11" ;
    gsndesign:page "11" .

gsndesign:src-0027
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0.4.1 (...) • The relationship between the argument and the context in which it is stated."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "11" ;
    gsndesign:page "11" .

gsndesign:src-0028
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0.4.11 (...) The goal structures also clearly document the context in which the claims of the argument are being put forward."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "15" ;
    gsndesign:page "15" .

gsndesign:src-0029
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:4.3 Where evidence is asserted to support the truth of the claim, this can be documented by providing a solution in GSN."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "12" ;
    gsndesign:page "12" .

gsndesign:src-0030
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Evidence
Information or objective artefacts being offered in support of one or more claims."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0031
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """0:4.3 Where evidence is asserted to support the truth of the claim, this can be documented by providing a solution in GSN.

Glossary: Evidence
Information or objective artefacts being offered in support of one or more claims."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "12; 128" ;
    gsndesign:page "12" ,
        "128" .

gsndesign:src-0032
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:4.6 Some claims and argument strategies are expressed in the context of assumptions. These assumptions must be valid for the claim or the strategy to be valid."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "12" ;
    gsndesign:page "12" .

gsndesign:src-0033
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """0:4.6 Some claims and argument strategies are expressed in the context of assumptions. These assumptions must be valid for the claim or the strategy to be valid.

1:6.3.6 Figure 1:6-1 depicts a dialectic challenge to a goal that results in defeat. The dialectic challenge within this structure asserts that if the evidence referred to in Solution CSn1 is valid, this is sufficient to establish that the claim in Goal G1 in the original structure is successfully challenged. Thus, a challenge to a target element is documented by identifying the counter evidence that makes this challenge."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.6" ,
        "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "12; 53" ;
    gsndesign:page "12" ,
        "53" .

gsndesign:src-0034
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:4.9 GSN provides two types of linkage between elements: SupportedBy and InContextOf. SupportedBy relationships – represented by lines with solid arrowheads – indicate inferential or evidential relationships between elements. InContextOf relationships – represented as lines with hollow arrowheads – declare contextual relationships."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.9" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "13" ;
    gsndesign:page "13" .

gsndesign:src-0035
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:4.10 When the elements of GSN are connected together, they are said to form a ‘goal structure’."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.10" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "13" ;
    gsndesign:page "13" .

gsndesign:src-0036
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:4.8 Goals, solutions, strategies, contexts, assumptions and justifications form the principal elements of GSN. (A full description of all GSN element-types is provided in Part 1: below)."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.8" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "13" ;
    gsndesign:page "13" .

gsndesign:src-0037
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0:4.11 Goal structures document the asserted chain of reasoning in the argument (through the visible decomposition of claimed goals and the description of argument strategies) and indicate how this argument is supported by evidence (through solutions)."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "0:4.11" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "15" ;
    gsndesign:page "15" .

gsndesign:src-0038
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.1.5 The core GSN elements defined here are intended to be combined to represent logical structures, known as ‘goal structures’."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.1.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" .

gsndesign:src-0039
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Evidential Relationship
A declared relationship between a claim and an evidence item by which the claim is substantiated."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0040
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Inferential Relationship
A declared inference between claims in the argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0041
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "0.3.2 At the heart of GSN is the explicit documentation of the hierarchy of claims and evidence. The top goal presents the overall claim asserted by the author and it is up to the reader to determine their belief that it is adequately supported."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "11" ;
    gsndesign:page "11" .

gsndesign:src-0042
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Glossary: Top Goal
A GSN Goal that presents the pinnacle claim in an argument. It is ‘top’ in terms of the argument hierarchy, rather than necessarily its physical layout. There may be more than one top goal in a GSN structure."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "128" ;
    gsndesign:page "128" .

gsndesign:src-0043
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1.1.1 (...) GSN defines elements, the allowable relationships between these elements and the acceptable language of the text within these elements."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "16" ;
    gsndesign:page "16" .

gsndesign:src-0044
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.1.2 Each element contains an element identifier. The identifier shall identify the element uniquely within an argument module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.1.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "16" ;
    gsndesign:page "16" .

gsndesign:src-0045
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-1 Core GSN Elements – Definition
A goal, rendered as a rectangle, presents a claim forming part of the argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "17" ;
    gsndesign:page "17" .

gsndesign:src-0046
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-1 Core GSN Elements – Definition
A strategy, rendered as a parallelogram, describes the inference that exists between a goal and its supporting goal(s)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "17" ;
    gsndesign:page "17" .

gsndesign:src-0047
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-1 Core GSN Elements – Definition
A solution, rendered as a circle, presents a reference to an evidence item."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "17" ;
    gsndesign:page "17" .

gsndesign:src-0048
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-1 Core GSN Elements – Definition
A context, rendered as shown left, presents a contextual artefact. This can be a reference to contextual information, or a statement."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "17" ;
    gsndesign:page "17" .

gsndesign:src-0049
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-1 Core GSN Elements – Definition
An assumption, rendered as an oval with the letter ‘A’ at the top- or bottom-right, presents an intentionally unsubstantiated statement."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "17" ;
    gsndesign:page "17" .

gsndesign:src-0050
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-1 Core GSN Elements – Definition
A justification, rendered as an oval with the letter ‘J’ at the top- or bottom-right, presents a statement of rationale."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" .

gsndesign:src-0051
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-1 Core GSN Elements – Definition
Undeveloped element decorator, rendered as a hollow diamond applied to the bottom centre of an element, indicates that a line of argument has not been developed."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" .

gsndesign:src-0052
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "It can apply to goals (as below) and strategies."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" .

gsndesign:src-0053
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-2 Core GSN Relationships - Definition
SupportedBy, rendered as a line with a solid arrowhead, allows support relationships between elements to be documented."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" .

gsndesign:src-0054
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Permitted ‘supported by’ connections are: goal-to-goal, goal-to-strategy, goal-to-solution, strategy to goal."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" .

gsndesign:src-0055
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Permitted ‘supported by’ connections are: goal-to-goal, goal-to-strategy, goal-to-solution, strategy to goal.

A module reference may be used in support and/or as context for an argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18; 34" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" ,
        "34" .

gsndesign:src-0056
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:2-2 Core GSN Relationships - Definition
InContextOf, rendered as a line with a hollow arrowhead, declares a contextual relationship."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" .

gsndesign:src-0057
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Permitted ‘in context of’ connections are: goal-to-context, goal-to-assumption, goal-to-justification, strategy-to-context, strategy-to-assumption and strategy-to-justification."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "18" ;
    gsndesign:page "18" .

gsndesign:src-0058
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.2 A GSN goal structure is a directed acyclic graph. This means that the graph does not allow loops, although one element can have multiple parents and children."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "19" ;
    gsndesign:page "19" .

gsndesign:src-0059
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "SupportedBy relationships shall not be constructed so as to directly or indirectly allow a goal to support itself."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "19" ;
    gsndesign:page "19" .

gsndesign:src-0060
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Similarly, InContextOf relationships shall not be constructed so as to directly or indirectly allow a goal to provide its own context."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "19" ;
    gsndesign:page "19" .

gsndesign:src-0061
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.4 (...) G1 may be referred to as the parent goal, whilst G2 and G3 would commonly be referred to as ‘supporting goals’, ‘sub-goals’ or ‘child goals’ of G1."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "19" ;
    gsndesign:page "19" .

gsndesign:src-0062
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.5 The structure shown in Figure 1:2-2 also asserts that if the claims presented in Goals G2 and G3 are true, this is sufficient to establish that the claim in Goal G1 is true."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:2.2.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "19" ;
    gsndesign:page "19" .

gsndesign:src-0063
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:2.2.7 Figure 1:2-4 represents the use of a reference to an evidence item to support a claim.
1:2.2.8 This structure represents an evidential relationship that asserts that the evidence referred to in the solution (Sn1) is sufficient to establish the truth of the claim made in the goal (G1)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:2.2.7" ,
        "1:2.2.8" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "21" ;
    gsndesign:page "21" .

gsndesign:src-0064
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.9 (...) It is noted that the evidential relationship between the goal and its supporting evidence is provided by the indivisible combination of the two ‘SupportedBy’ relationships."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.9" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "22" ;
    gsndesign:page "22" .

gsndesign:src-0065
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.10 Claims can only be asserted to be true in a specified context. Context elements can be used in GSN to make this relationship clear."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.10" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "22" ;
    gsndesign:page "22" .

gsndesign:src-0066
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.11 Where used, contexts define or constrain the scope over which the claim is made. Since a contextual statement makes an assertion in the argument structure, nothing in the supporting argument for the goal to which the context is applied should contradict or undermine the relationship between the goal and the context."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.11" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "22" ;
    gsndesign:page "22" .

gsndesign:src-0067
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.16 As before, since a contextual statement makes an assertion in the argument structure, nothing in the supporting argument deriving from the strategy to which the context is applied should contradict or undermine the relationship between the strategy and the context."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.16" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "23" ;
    gsndesign:page "23" .

gsndesign:src-0068
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.11 (...) Context is taken to be connected to the entirety of the argument supporting the referenced element. Therefore, it is not necessary to restate the context in the supporting argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.11" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "22" ;
    gsndesign:page "22" .

gsndesign:src-0069
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.16 (...) Context is taken to be connected to the entirety of the argument supporting the referenced element. Therefore, it is not necessary to restate the context in the supporting argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.16" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "23" ;
    gsndesign:page "23" .

gsndesign:src-0070
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.12 An assumption applied to a goal declares an assumption made in stating the claim. The meaning of the structure in Figure 1:2-7 is that the claim in goal G1 is asserted in a context where the assumption A1 is true."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:2.2.12" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "22" ;
    gsndesign:page "22" .

gsndesign:src-0071
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.13 An assumption is an unsubstantiated statement. Having connected an assumption to a goal G1, the assumption is taken to be connected to the entirety of the relevant argument. Therefore, it is not necessary to restate the assumption in the relevant argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.13" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "23" ;
    gsndesign:page "23" .

gsndesign:src-0072
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.17 (...) Having connected an assumption to a strategy S1, the assumption is taken to be connected to the entirety of the argument resulting from S1. Therefore, it is not necessary to restate the assumption in the supporting argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.17" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "24" ;
    gsndesign:page "24" .

gsndesign:src-0073
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.14 Figure 1:2-8 shows the connection of a justification to a goal. A justification does not alter the meaning of the claim made in the goal, but provides rationale for its inclusion or its phrasing. Unlike assumptions, justifications are not taken to be connected to the entirety of the argument supporting the referenced goal. They are local to the element to which they are linked. Should an equivalent justification be required elsewhere in the argument, it will need to be re stated or re-linked."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:2.2.14" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "23" ;
    gsndesign:page "23" .

gsndesign:src-0074
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.19 A justification applies to the element to which it is connected. Should an equivalent justification be required elsewhere in the argument, it will need to be re-stated or re-linked."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.19" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "24" ;
    gsndesign:page "24" .

gsndesign:src-0075
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.20 GSN structures can become large and it is often convenient to illustrate fragments of the argument structure in separate diagrams. To be able to convey that the argument continues in, or is a continuation from, a separate diagram an optional ‘off diagram’ decorator may be applied to elements at the top or bottom of each diagram."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.2.20" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "24" ;
    gsndesign:page "24" .

gsndesign:src-0076
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.2.20 GSN structures can become large and it is often convenient to illustrate fragments of the argument structure in separate diagrams. To be able to convey that the argument continues in, or is a continuation from, a separate diagram an optional ‘off diagram’ decorator may be applied to elements at the top or bottom of each diagram. Typically these will be goal or strategy elements. | Figure 1:2-12 ‘Off-Diagram’ Decorators"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:2.2.20" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "24; 25" ;
    gsndesign:page "24" ,
        "25" .

gsndesign:src-0077
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.3.1 This section presents a series of simple rules which govern the grammatical structure of statements used in GSN elements."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" .

gsndesign:src-0078
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:2.3.1 This section presents a series of simple rules which govern the grammatical structure of statements used in GSN elements.

1:5.2.3 (…) The corresponding argument could be located in a paragraph of accompanying text, a goal in the local argument, or a goal in a separate module."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.1" ,
        "1:5.2.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25; 47" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" ,
        "47" .

gsndesign:src-0079
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.3.2 GSN goals document the claims made in the argument (i.e. premises and conclusions). Each goal shall contain a single goal statement, expressed as a proposition in the form of a noun phrase + verb-phrase sentence."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" .

gsndesign:src-0080
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.3.3 GSN strategy statements describe the reasoning that connects parent goals and their supporting goals, but the core claims and the structure connecting those claims remain unchanged. Strategy statements contain a brief description of the argument approach."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" .

gsndesign:src-0081
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.3.4 GSN solutions make no claim, but are simply references to evidence items that provide support for a particular claim. They shall therefore be stated as noun-phrases."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" .

gsndesign:src-0082
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.3.5 Two kinds of GSN context statement exist. Where a context statement is a reference to an artefact of some kind, which informs the reasoning step, the context statement shall be expressed as a noun phrase."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" .

gsndesign:src-0083
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.3.5 (...) Where a context statement draws attention to explanatory contextual information (such as the definition of some term), this information shall be stated briefly using complete sentences of a noun-phrase + verb-phrase structure."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" .

gsndesign:src-0084
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.3.6 GSN assumptions and justifications provide additional information necessary for the correct understanding of the argument. This information is stated as fully as necessary, using complete sentences in the form noun phrase + verb phrase."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" .

gsndesign:src-0085
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:2.3.4 GSN solutions make no claim, but are simply references to evidence items that provide support for a particular claim. (…) 1:2.3.5 (…) Where a context statement is a reference to an artefact of some kind, which informs the reasoning step, the context statement shall be expressed as a noun-phrase."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.3.4" ,
        "1:2.3.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "25" ;
    gsndesign:page "25" .

gsndesign:src-0086
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.1.3 In cases where the elements defined in the following sections are used in the development of instantiations of the patterns to produce individual assurance arguments, it is important to ensure that they are all removed, or instantiated, in the final, delivered, version of the argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.1.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "26" ;
    gsndesign:page "26" .

gsndesign:src-0087
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.1.3 (...) By exception, a final, delivered, version of the argument may be provided in a form that includes instantiable elements together with instantiation data as defined in section 1:3.5."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.1.3" ,
        "1:3.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "26" ;
    gsndesign:page "26" .

gsndesign:src-0088
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.2.1 This section describes the extensions to GSN defined in order to support two aspects of structural abstraction: • Multiplicity – generalised n-ary relationships between GSN elements;"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.2.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "26" ;
    gsndesign:page "26" .

gsndesign:src-0089
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.2.1 (...) • Optionality – optional and alternative relationships between GSN elements."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.2.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "26" ;
    gsndesign:page "26" .

gsndesign:src-0090
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.2.2 Table 1:3-1 illustrates the extensions made to GSN to facilitate the representation of multiplicity. These symbols are defined for use as decorators on all existing GSN relation types."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ,
        "1:3.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "26" ;
    gsndesign:page "26" .

gsndesign:src-0091
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.2.2 (…) Multiplicity symbols can be used to describe how many instances of one element-type relate to another element."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "26" ;
    gsndesign:page "26" .

gsndesign:src-0092
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.1.3 In cases where the elements defined in the following sections are used in the development of instantiations of the patterns to produce individual assurance arguments, it is important to ensure that they are all removed, or instantiated, in the final, delivered, version of the argument. By exception, a final, delivered, version of the argument may be provided in a form that includes instantiable elements together with instantiation data as defined in section 1:3.5."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.1.3" ,
        "1:3.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "26" ;
    gsndesign:page "26" .

gsndesign:src-0093
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
A solid ball is the symbol for multiple instantiations."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0094
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
The optional label next to the ball indicates the cardinality of the relationship. It can be expressed as an instantiable parameter relevant to the argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0095
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
If no label is included then the cardinality can be any value from one upwards."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0096
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
If cardinality from zero onwards is required this should be explicitly declared e.g. 0..x declares that there may be zero to x branches (inclusive). It could also be written as 0≤n≤x."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0097
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
A hollow ball indicates ‘optional’ instantiation,"""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0098
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
Optional instantiation means that the relationship and the argument below may or may not be instantiated."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0099
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
A solid diamond is the symbol for Choice."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0100
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
The optional label next to the diamond indicates the cardinality of the relationship."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0101
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
It can be expressed as an instantiable parameter relevant to the argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0102
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-1 – Definition
If no label is included then the cardinality can be any value from one to the number of supporting elements."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0103
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.2.3 The extension to GSN shown in Figure 1:3-1 shows the representation of structural choice A GSN choice can be used to denote possible alternatives in satisfying a relationship."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ,
        "1:3.2.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "27" ;
    gsndesign:page "27" .

gsndesign:src-0104
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-2 – Definition
[TRIANGLE]
This decorator denotes that the attached element remains to be instantiated, i.e. at some later stage the ‘abstract’ element needs to be replaced (instantiated) with a more concrete instance."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "28" ;
    gsndesign:page "28" .

gsndesign:src-0105
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-2 – Definition
This decorator can be applied to any GSN element type, and should be applied to the bottom centre of the element."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "28" ;
    gsndesign:page "28" .

gsndesign:src-0106
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-2 – Definition
The token to be instantiated is contained within curly brackets."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "28" ;
    gsndesign:page "28" .

gsndesign:src-0107
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-2 – Definition
Decorators can be overlaid to denote that the attached element requires both further development and instantiation. The ‘undeveloped’ decorator was introduced in Table 1:2-1."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "28" ;
    gsndesign:page "28" .

gsndesign:src-0108
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-2 – Definition
This combined decorator can be applied to any GSN element that the undeveloped decorator can be applied to, and should be applied to the bottom centre of the element."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "28" ;
    gsndesign:page "28" .

gsndesign:src-0109
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.4.1 A Pattern is not just the collection of GSN symbols."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "28" ;
    gsndesign:page "28" .

gsndesign:src-0110
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.4.1 (…) Additionally there should always be a supporting pattern description that defines the underlying intent and constraints on its use."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "28, 29" ;
    gsndesign:page "28" ,
        "29" .

gsndesign:src-0111
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.4.1 (…) The format and presentation of the definition is not prescribed by this standard."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0112
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.4.1 (…) A pattern catalogue may be created to collate a series of patterns; where such a catalogue is created the structure and format of the definition should be consistent and each pattern’s definition should have a unique {pattern identifier}."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0113
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.4.1 (…) The following topics should be addressed in the pattern definition:"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0114
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.1 Name
1:3.4.2 The pattern’s name is the label by which the pattern can be identified and should meaningfully communicate the principle argument being presented."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.1" ,
        "1:3.4.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0115
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.4.2 (…) It may be accompanied by one or more aliases, which are an alternative identifiers by which the pattern may also be referred to."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0116
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.2  Intent
1:3.4.3 The intent statement should state clearly what the pattern aims to achieve."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.2" ,
        "1:3.4.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0117
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.3 Motivation
1:3.4.4 The motivation statement can be used to state why the pattern was created. It could be expressed in terms of previous experiences e.g. as the abstraction of a successfully presented argument, or challenges addressed e.g. argument topics that are often incompletely or poorly addressed."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.3" ,
        "1:3.4.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0118
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.4 Structure
1:3.4.5 The structure uses the structural and element abstraction notations to present the pattern, clearly indicating where the argument needs to be further developed or populated with details to instantiate the pattern for a specific case."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.4" ,
        "1:3.4.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0119
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.5 Participants
1:3.4.6 The participants section augments the structure by providing a description of each element. This can provide more complete descriptions, clarify the role of the element in the overall argument and emphasise the aspects that require development or instantiation."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.5" ,
        "1:3.4.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0120
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.6 Collaboration
1:3.4.7 The collaboration section should describe how elements of the pattern work together to achieve the desired effect, particularly where there are links that are not readily apparent from the argument structure."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.6" ,
        "1:3.4.7" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "29" ;
    gsndesign:page "29" .

gsndesign:src-0121
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.7 Applicability
1:3.4.8 The applicability section should state under what circumstances the pattern can be applied, making clear the assumptions and principles underlying the pattern to avoid inappropriate application in a mismatched context. This section should record what contextual information is required in order to apply the pattern."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.7" ,
        "1:3.4.8" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "30" ;
    gsndesign:page "30" .

gsndesign:src-0122
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.8 Consequences
1:3.4.9 The consequences section should make clear what work remains after the pattern has been applied. This should highlight where further support to the argument is required, and assumptions that need to be discharged."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.8" ,
        "1:3.4.9" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "30" ;
    gsndesign:page "30" .

gsndesign:src-0123
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.9 Implementation
1:3.4.10 The implementation section should communicate how the application of the pattern is carried out e.g. the order in which elements should be developed; communicate hints or techniques that may ease successful application; highlight common or recognised pitfalls with the application of the pattern; and record potential misinterpretation of the terms or concepts in the pattern."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.10" ,
        "1:3.4.9" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "30" ;
    gsndesign:page "30" .

gsndesign:src-0124
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.10 Examples
1:3.4.11 It may be useful to provide example illustrations of the application of the pattern, particularly for more abstract patterns. Illustrations should include a typical case and can be supplemented with atypical cases where more than one example is provided."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.10" ,
        "1:3.4.11" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "30" ;
    gsndesign:page "30" .

gsndesign:src-0125
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.11 Known uses
1:3.4.12 It may be useful to provide references to known applications of the pattern. These can serve as additional examples."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.11" ,
        "1:3.4.12" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "30" ;
    gsndesign:page "30" .

gsndesign:src-0126
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:3.4.12 Related patterns
1:3.4.13 This section can be used to reference patterns that are related e.g. addressing the same intent in a different context."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.4.12" ,
        "1:3.4.13" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "30" ;
    gsndesign:page "30" .

gsndesign:src-0127
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.1 By exception, as an alternative to the obligation to instantiate all patterns elements a completed argument, instantiation can be by means a ‘template argument’ together with instantiation data. This can avoid producing multiple pages of GSN structure where the argument structure is highly repetitive when repeated over multiple aspects e.g. where an argument over requirement satisfaction for individual requirements appeals to different test cases, but otherwise is an identical argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.5.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "30, 31" ;
    gsndesign:page "30" ,
        "31" .

gsndesign:src-0128
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.1 (…) Table 1:3-3 identifies an additional symbol used to indicate that the GSN argument is a template argument to be instantiated from instantiation data."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ,
        "1:3.5.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0129
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.2 A template argument is a special case of a pattern argument. It uses the core GSN and argument pattern extension to construct an argument structure which requires no further development."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.5.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0130
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.2 (…) The use of the ‘undeveloped’ decorator is not permitted within a template argument in its published form."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.5.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0131
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.2 (…) The instantiation data must cover all instantiable aspects including optionality, multiplicity and choice."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.5.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0132
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.3 Where a template argument ends at an element other than a solution, that final element must exist elsewhere within the argument,"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.5.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0133
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.3 (…) or in the case of an away element, must be declared in instantiated form in the module interface."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.5.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0134
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.4 Where a template argument and instantiation data is used, it must be possible to apply the instantiation, creating all instantiated versions and meet all the core GSN rules, including uniqueness of element identifiers."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.5.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0135
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:3.5.4 (…) Uninstantiated identifiers in a template only need to be unique within the template."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3.5.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0136
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-3 – Definition
Instantiation Data Reference. This symbol indicates that the GSN argument below the attached element is to be instantiated as a template argument. It provides a reference to the information used to instantiate the template argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0137
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-3 – Definition
The symbol is not considered a GSN element as it does not form part of the argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0138
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:3-3 – Definition
It is attached to the top element of the template argument by a dotted line between the top edges of that element and the symbol (as shown below)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "31" ;
    gsndesign:page "31" .

gsndesign:src-0139
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.1.1 Goal structures can be partitioned into separate, but interrelated, modules. This can allow the division of an overall goal structure into separate goal structures focusing on particular aspects of the overall argument. This section describes how GSN has been extended to represent modular arguments."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.1.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "32" ;
    gsndesign:page "32" .

gsndesign:src-0140
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.1.3 A module may contain one or more arguments and may contain other modules."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.1.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "32" ;
    gsndesign:page "32" .

gsndesign:src-0141
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.1.2 The concepts of ‘argument view’, ‘architecture view’, module interfaces and inter-module contracts are introduced."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.1.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "32" ;
    gsndesign:page "32" .

gsndesign:src-0142
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.2.1 The argument view depicts the argument inside an individual module. The following elements are used in addition to the core GSN notation: • Away Goal; • Away Solution;  • Away Context; • Away Assumption; • Away Justification. • Module Reference; • Contract Reference"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.2.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "32" ;
    gsndesign:page "32" .

gsndesign:src-0143
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.2.1 The argument view depicts the argument inside an individual module. The following elements are used in addition to the core GSN notation: • Away Goal; • Away Solution;  • Away Context; • Away Assumption; • Away Justification. • Module Reference; • Contract Reference"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.2.1" .

gsndesign:src-0144
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.2.2 (…) Note that each argument module has its own namespace for identifiers, thus two elements with the same element identifier can exist in different argument modules."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "32" ;
    gsndesign:page "32" .

gsndesign:src-0145
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.2.2 (…) Element identifiers must be unique within a single argument module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "32" ;
    gsndesign:page "32" .

gsndesign:src-0146
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
An away goal reference is rendered as a rectangle with a bisecting line in the lower half of the rectangle. The area in the lower portion contains a miniature shaded module element symbol."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0147
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
An away solution, rendered as a semi-circle sitting on top of a rectangle (the semi-circle may be raised above the rectangle by extending its vertical extremes in a straight line)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0148
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
An away context, rendered as shown left, repeats a contextual artefact."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0149
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
An away assumption, rendered as a semi-ellipse sitting on top of a rectangle with the letter ‘A’ at the top-right (the semi-ellipse may be raised above the rectangle by extending its vertical extremes in a straight line)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0150
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
An away justification, rendered as a semi-ellipse sitting on top of a rectangle with the letter ‘J’ at the top-right (the semi-ellipse may be raised above the rectangle by extending its vertical extremes in a straight line)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0151
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "An away goal reference repeats a claim presented in another argument module."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0152
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "An away solution repeats a reference to evidence items presented in another argument module."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0153
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "An away context repeats a reference to context presented in another argument module."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0154
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "An away assumption repeats an assumption presented in another argument module and is typically used only in Contract Modules."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0155
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "An away justification repeats a justification presented in another argument module and is typically used only in Contract Modules."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0156
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "For all away elements defined above, the element has an identifier which is the {element identifier} of the referenced element in the module in which it was originally declared."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0157
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "The <element statement> contains an exact repetition of the text of the referenced element."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0158
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "The {module identifier} is the identifier of the module in which the referenced element occurs."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "33" ;
    gsndesign:page "33" .

gsndesign:src-0159
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
A module reference, rendered as a rectangle with a second smaller rectangle adjoining at the top left, presents a reference to a module containing an argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0160
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Note that a module reference points to the totality of the argument contained in the referenced argument module, rather than just to an individual claim."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0161
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "A module reference may be used in support and/or as context for an argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0162
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "A module reference cannot be used within a contract module."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0163
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
A contract reference, rendered as a rectangle with a two smaller rectangles (of equal size to each other) adjoining at the top left and bottom right, presents a reference to a contract module."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0164
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Note that a contract reference points to the totality of the relationship contained in the referenced contract module, rather than just to an individual claim."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0165
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "A contract reference cannot be used within a contract module."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0166
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
Public Decorator, rendered as a miniature module symbol and superimposed on a goal, solution, context, assumption or justification symbol at the top right."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0167
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "This indicates that the element is publicly visible in one or more interfaces of the module and can be referenced as an away element."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0168
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "The preferred location of the public decorator is within the element shape. Where this is not practical (e.g. as shown below) the exact positioning of the public decorator is not important as long as the association with the element is clear."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "34" ;
    gsndesign:page "34" .

gsndesign:src-0169
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-1 – Definition
To be supported by contract: This decorator, attached centrally immediately below the goal to which it relates, denotes that support for the claim presented by the attached goal is intended to be provided from an argument in another module linked by an as-yet-undisclosed contract."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "35" ;
    gsndesign:page "35" .

gsndesign:src-0170
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "At some later stage, the element may be updated to replace this decorator with support from a named contract module, or may be left as it is, with the necessary support defined in a higher-level argument’s architecture view."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "35" ;
    gsndesign:page "35" .

gsndesign:src-0171
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "This decorator can only be applied to goal elements, and can be used in conjunction with the ‘to be instantiated’ annotation, but is mutually exclusive with the ‘to be developed’ annotation."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "35" ;
    gsndesign:page "35" .

gsndesign:src-0172
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "This decorator cannot be used within a contract module."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "35" ;
    gsndesign:page "35" .

gsndesign:src-0173
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-2 – Definition – SupportedBy
In addition to the permitted connections defined in the core GSN definition (See Table 1:2-2), in modular GSN the following ‘supported by’ connections are permitted: goal-to-away_goal, goal-to-away_solution, goal-to-module_reference, goal-to-contract_reference, strategy to away_goal."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" .

gsndesign:src-0174
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "In a Contract module, the following ‘supported by’ connections are also permitted: away_goal-to-goal, away_goal-to-strategy, away_goal-to-away_goal."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" .

gsndesign:src-0175
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-2 – Definition – InContextOf
In addition to the permitted connections defined in the core GSN definition (See Table 1:2-2), in modular GSN the following ‘in context of’ connections are permitted: goal-to-away_goal, goal-to-away_context, goal-to-away_assumption, goal-to-away_justification, goal-to-module_reference, strategy-to-away_goal, strategy-to-away_context, strategy-to-away_assumption, strategy-to-away_justification and strategy-to-module_reference."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" .

gsndesign:src-0176
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-2 – Definition – InContextOf
In addition to the permitted connections defined in the core GSN definition (See Table 1:2-2), in modular GSN the following ‘in context of’ connections are permitted: goal-to-away_goal, goal-to-away_context, goal-to-away_assumption, goal-to-away_justification, goal-to-module_reference, strategy-to-away_goal, strategy-to-away_context, strategy-to-away_assumption, strategy-to-away_justification and strategy-to-module_reference.

Table 1:2-1 Core GSN Elements – Definition
A strategy, rendered as a parallelogram, describes the inference that exists between a goal and its supporting goal(s).

Permitted ‘in context of’ connections are: goal-to-context, goal-to-assumption, goal-to-justification, strategy-to-context, strategy-to-assumption and strategy-to-justification."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36; 17; 18" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" ,
        "17" ,
        "18" .

gsndesign:src-0177
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-2 – Definition – InContextOf
In addition to the permitted connections defined in the core GSN definition (See Table 1:2-2), in modular GSN the following ‘in context of’ connections are permitted: goal-to-away_goal, goal-to-away_context, goal-to-away_assumption, goal-to-away_justification, goal-to-module_reference, strategy-to-away_goal, strategy-to-away_context, strategy-to-away_assumption, strategy-to-away_justification and strategy-to-module_reference.

Permitted ‘in context of’ connections are: goal-to-context, goal-to-assumption, goal-to-justification, strategy-to-context, strategy-to-assumption and strategy-to-justification."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2" ,
        "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36; 18" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" ,
        "18" .

gsndesign:src-0178
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "In a Contract module, the following ‘in context of’ connections are also permitted: away_goal-to-away_context, away_goal-to-away_assumption, away_goal-to-away_justification."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" .

gsndesign:src-0179
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.1 The GSN elements defined in Sections 1:2.1 and 1:4.2 above are intended to be combined to represent logical structures. The notation interpretation for core elements within modular extensions is unchanged. Away goals, away solutions and away context elements are used in place of their core counterparts with the addition that they are references to the goal, solution or context in the referenced argument module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.1" ,
        "1:4.2" ,
        "1:4.3.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" .

gsndesign:src-0180
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.2 Away goals cannot be (hierarchically) decomposed and further supported by sub-elements within the current argument module; rather, decomposition needs to occur within the referenced argument module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.3.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" .

gsndesign:src-0181
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.2 (…) By exception, it is valid to decompose away goals within safety case contract modules where they refer to a goal requiring support from a contract module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.3.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "36, 37" ;
    gsndesign:page "36" ,
        "37" .

gsndesign:src-0182
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.2 (…) Conversely, the goal requiring support, which is addressed via a contract, must not be decomposed in its host module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.3.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "37" ;
    gsndesign:page "37" .

gsndesign:src-0183
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.3 Arguments supported by another argument module can be indicated in a number of ways. Figure 1:4-1 illustrates a firm relationship by which the parent goal is supported by a specific goal in the referenced argument module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ,
        "1:4.3.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "37" ;
    gsndesign:page "37" .

gsndesign:src-0184
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.3 (…) As with core GSN, an intermediate strategy could be shown and the parent goal/strategy could be supported by one or more argument elements in addition to the away goal."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.3.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "37" ;
    gsndesign:page "37" .

gsndesign:src-0185
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.4 By making the relationship to the away goal the author is asserting not only the inference of support for the parent goal, but also that the context in which the away goal is declared is consistent with the context and assumptions in scope for the parent goal."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.3.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "37" ;
    gsndesign:page "37" .

gsndesign:src-0186
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.6 An alternative approach is illustrated in Figure 1:4-3. The contract module instantiating the support relationship is not specified. Here, the relevant higher-level argument abstraction (e.g. architecture view) should be referred to, which will indicate where the required contract details are specified."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ,
        "1:4.3.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "38" ;
    gsndesign:page "38" .

gsndesign:src-0187
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.3.8 There may be occasions when a goal or strategy requires fuller justification than can be provided within the confines of a GSN justification element (described in Section 1:2.1 above). In such cases, an away goal can be substituted for the justification. This enables the author to invoke the argument supporting the away goal in the remote argument module as context for the goal or strategy they are currently working with."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:2.1" ,
        "1:4.3.8" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "38" ;
    gsndesign:page "38" .

gsndesign:src-0188
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.4.1 The architecture view provides an abstract view of the relationship between argument modules."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.4.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0189
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.4.1 (…) The use of links in the architecture view is extended and there is a clear distinction between the use of SupportedBy and InContextOf relationships between individual elements within modules and their use in the architecture view."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.4.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0190
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-3 – Definition – Module
Module symbols are used in the architecture view to represent an argument module."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0191
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "The module identifier may be located internal to the symbol (as shown) or immediately below the symbol."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0192
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Inclusion of the module description is optional"@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0193
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-3 – Definition – Contract
Contract symbols are used in the architecture view to represent a special type of module that defines the relationship between argument module interfaces and shows how one module supports the argument in another."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0194
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Alternative contract module symbols are available to suit different styles of presentation of the architecture."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0195
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "The contract identifier may be located internal to the symbol (as shown) or immediately below the symbol. Where the simple form symbol is used the identifier may be located to the side of the symbol."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0196
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Inclusion of the contract description is optional."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "39" ;
    gsndesign:page "39" .

gsndesign:src-0197
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-3 – Definition – Module
A Module Interface Connector, rendered as a small square on the boundary of a module symbol, can optionally be added to aid clarity of the specific interface (specified by the {interface identifier}) used by the inter-module relationship."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "40" ;
    gsndesign:page "40" .

gsndesign:src-0198
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Where no interface is declared the default interface is assumed."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "40" ;
    gsndesign:page "40" .

gsndesign:src-0199
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-4 – Definition
The ModuleSupportedBy and ModuleInContextOf relationships are used in the architecture view represent one or more support/context relationship(s) between the elements within the modules."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "40" ;
    gsndesign:page "40" .

gsndesign:src-0200
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-4 – Definition
Note that the use of these symbols in the architecture View differs from that within the argument view. In the architecture view the asserted relationship is between modules and may reflect multiple individual support/context relationships across the modules’ interfaces."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "40" ;
    gsndesign:page "40" .

gsndesign:src-0201
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-4 – Definition
The Composite Relationship is used where both a supported and a context relationship exists between modules."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "40" ;
    gsndesign:page "40" .

gsndesign:src-0202
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:4-4 – Definition
The support/context relationships between modules may be bidirectional, and therefore the relationship may be shown with any of the support, context or composite arrow at either end and in any combination. A small selection of the possible combinations are illustrated (4 out of the possible 9 in addition to the 3 single ended variants)"""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "40" ;
    gsndesign:page "40" .

gsndesign:src-0203
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.5.1 It is useful to represent the abstracted structure of an argument in an architecture view. The process of abstraction hides the detailed structure of the argument. Goals, strategies, solutions and context are not shown in the architecture view; instead, just the modules and their relationships are depicted. The relationships are summarised such that rather than using separate links for each pairing of elements between the modules, only one link is shown."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.5.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "40, 41" ;
    gsndesign:page "40" ,
        "41" .

gsndesign:src-0204
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.5.2 Figure 1:4-6 shows a SupportedBy relationship between modules. The relationship indicates that there exists one or more goal and/or strategy within module 1 which is supported by one or more goal(s) and/or evidence elements within module 2, and similarly for modules 1 and 3. There is no inference that the supporting argument provided in modules 2 and 3 necessarily supports the same goal in module 1."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ,
        "1:4.5.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "41" ;
    gsndesign:page "41" .

gsndesign:src-0205
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.5.2 (…) It is entirely permissible for a module both to provide support, and to be supported by another module, provided that this does not create circularity within the argument established by the composed argument modules."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.5.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "41" ;
    gsndesign:page "41" .

gsndesign:src-0206
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.5.3 Contract modules can be used in the support relationship between modules to aid decoupling as shown in Figure 1:4-7. Both the full and simple forms of the contract module symbol are shown for comparison. An architecture view may use either form but should be self-consistent."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4" ,
        "1:4.5.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "41" ;
    gsndesign:page "41" .

gsndesign:src-0207
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "The de-coupling by use of a contract permits argument module construction in cases where the eventual source of support for an argument is unknown at the time of authoring or can be changed for example through re-use or planned product improvement or reconfiguration."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "41" ;
    gsndesign:page "41" .

gsndesign:src-0208
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.5.5 The addition of module interface connectors can aid visualisation of which particular interface is used for a particular inter-module relationship and can be used to give greater clarity where multiple relationships exist between modules."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.5.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "42" ;
    gsndesign:page "42" .

gsndesign:src-0209
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.1 A Module Interface identifies the published elements of the argument that a module contains."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "43" ;
    gsndesign:page "43" .

gsndesign:src-0210
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.2 A Module may have one or more interfaces defined, each of which should have a unique {interface identifier}."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "43" ;
    gsndesign:page "43" .

gsndesign:src-0211
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "The default interface publishes all public goals and relevant context together with all goals and references requiring external support."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "43" ;
    gsndesign:page "43" .

gsndesign:src-0212
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Other interfaces may be published to suit specific purposes and these may be more restrictive than the default interface e.g. to allow detail to be hidden for simplicity or to control exposure of details necessary to relate to peer modules, but unnecessary for integration into a higher level argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "43" ;
    gsndesign:page "43" .

gsndesign:src-0213
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 The Module Interface by default contains the following elements; each GSN element should be stated in full including element identifier and the complete element statement:"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "44" ;
    gsndesign:page "44" .

gsndesign:src-0214
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) 1. The module and interface identifier, description and configuration information."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "44" ;
    gsndesign:page "44" .

gsndesign:src-0215
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) 2. The goal(s) addressed by the module. These are all the goals declared public using the public decorator within the module. These are not necessarily the ‘top’ goals of a module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "44" ;
    gsndesign:page "44" .

gsndesign:src-0216
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) 3. Goals requiring support. This should include all those indicated as ‘to be supported by contract’ and any goals requiring support where an explicit dependency has not been declared."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "44" ;
    gsndesign:page "44" .

gsndesign:src-0217
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) 4. The contextual elements (context, assumptions and justifications) relevant to the goals defined above (2 and 3)."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "44" ;
    gsndesign:page "44" .

gsndesign:src-0218
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) The interface needs to include all relevant contextual element in scope for that goal, which may be more than the context directly linked to the goal in the argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "44" ;
    gsndesign:page "44" .

gsndesign:src-0219
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) Any contextual element included as in scope of a goal in the interface needs to be made public, even if not intended for reference by another argument module. Contextual elements are specific to each goal."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "44, 45" ;
    gsndesign:page "44" ,
        "45" .

gsndesign:src-0220
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) 5. Solutions and context that are available to be cited in support of goals in other argument modules. This includes all solutions and context declared public within the module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0221
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) 6. Dependencies explicitly referenced within the module. This includes all away-goal, away-solution, away-contextual element references used by the argument within the module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0222
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.3 (…) It also includes module(s) and contract module(s) referenced from within the module, together with the goals supported by them."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0223
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.4 Where a module interface is declared that is a subset of the default interface the sub-set should include all related contextual elements for any goals that are included."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0224
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.5 Where a module contains other modules, the interface for the containing module can contain any element of the interface of any of the contained modules, in effect promoting the element from the contained module interface to the containing module interface."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0225
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.5 (…) Where such a promotion occurs, this should ensure that the associated contextual elements for promoted goals are also promoted."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0226
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.6 The identifiers for all elements within an interface, including that for any promoted element must be unique."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0227
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.6 (…) Where potential duplication occurs, e.g. where goals of the same identifier are promoted from two contained modules, this can be achieved by including the relevant module identifier, or by introducing an alias for the promoted element."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0228
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.6.7 The default interface should maintain full traceability between promoted elements and their originating module, but this does not have to be carried through to an interface that is published for a specific purpose. This abstraction allows an interface to be published without revealing the internal structure of the argument it contains."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.6.7" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0229
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.7.1 A contract may be used to relate the interfaces of modules to show how the arguments in one module support another."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.7.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0230
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.7.1 (…) A contract may be described in textual form (e.g. as a table) or for more complex relationships may be described within a contract module using GSN."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.7.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "45" ;
    gsndesign:page "45" .

gsndesign:src-0231
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.7.2 A contract module is a special type of module that controls the relationship between argument module interfaces using arguments to define how a goal in one module is supported by one or more goals in one or more other modules."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.7.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" .

gsndesign:src-0232
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.7.2 (…) It also enables argument to justify the consistency of context between those goals."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.7.2" .

gsndesign:src-0233
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.7.3 As the contract module’s purpose is to define the relationship between module interfaces it does not have a module interface of its own and cannot publish public elements."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.7.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" .

gsndesign:src-0234
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.7.3 (…) All references from the contract module to elements in argument modules must be made using away elements (e.g. away goal, away solution, away context) and can only be made to elements that exist in module interfaces that have been made visible to it."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.7.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" .

gsndesign:src-0235
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:4.7.4 A contract module can contain other modules, however the interfaces for these contained modules are only be available to the contract module in which they are contained, and/or to other modules within the same scope, i.e. they are private to the containing contract module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:4.7.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" .

gsndesign:src-0236
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.1.1 An Assurance Claim Point (ACP) can be used in GSN to indicate that a confidence argument is associated with an assertion in a risk argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.1.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" .

gsndesign:src-0237
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:5.1.1 An Assurance Claim Point (ACP) can be used in GSN to indicate that a confidence argument is associated with an assertion in a risk argument.

2:11.2.2 Dialectic can be used as a prefix:
• ‘Dialectic argument’ - the outcome of using dialectic thinking or process"""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.1.1" ,
        "2:11.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46; 115" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" ,
        "115" .

gsndesign:src-0238
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:5.1.1 (…) For each ACP there should exist a corresponding confidence argument.

1:5.2.1 Table 1:5-1 illustrates the extensions made to GSN to facilitate the representation of ACPs. These symbols are defined for use as decorators on all core GSN relation types.

1:5.3.1 The presence of an ACP indicates that a separate confidence argument documenting the reasons for having confidence in the relationship or referenced artefact is provided. The nature of confidence arguments is discussed in detail in [8] (Risk, Confidence and Compliance Arguments)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ,
        "1:5.1.1" ,
        "1:5.2.1" ,
        "1:5.3.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46; 47, 48" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" ,
        "47" ,
        "48" .

gsndesign:src-0239
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:5.1.1 (…) For each ACP there should exist a corresponding confidence argument.

1:5.3.1 The presence of an ACP indicates that a separate confidence argument documenting the reasons for having confidence in the relationship or referenced artefact is provided. The nature of confidence arguments is discussed in detail in [8] (Risk, Confidence and Compliance Arguments)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.1.1" ,
        "1:5.3.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46; 47, 48" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" ,
        "47" ,
        "48" .

gsndesign:src-0240
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.2.1 Table 1:5-1 illustrates the extensions made to GSN to facilitate the representation of ACPs. These symbols are defined for use as decorators on all core GSN relation types."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ,
        "1:5.2.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" .

gsndesign:src-0241
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.2.2 ACPs may also be added to any element of an argument that provides a reference to an artefact e.g. solution or context where there is a need to argue the confidence in the artefact that the element references rather than the confidence related to its relationship to the argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "47" ;
    gsndesign:page "47" .

gsndesign:src-0242
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:5-1 – Definition 
A solid square is the symbol for ACP used as a decorator for a relationship. (...) It can be applied to ‘SupportedBy’ and ‘InContextOf’ relationships."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" .

gsndesign:src-0243
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:5-2 – Definition 
A solid square is the symbol for ACP used as a decorator for an element. (...) It can be applied as a decorator to elements that make reference to an artefact (e.g. solution, context)."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "47" ;
    gsndesign:page "47" .

gsndesign:src-0244
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:5-1 – Definition 
The label next to the square indicates the ACP identifier."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "46" ;
    gsndesign:page "46" .

gsndesign:src-0245
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:5-2 – Definition 
The label next to the square indicates the ACP identifier."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "47" ;
    gsndesign:page "47" .

gsndesign:src-0246
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:5-2 – Definition 
The ACP decorator can be combined with the ‘uninstantiated’ decorator."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "47" ;
    gsndesign:page "47" .

gsndesign:src-0247
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.2.3 Each ACP should have a unique identifier, e.g. “ACP1”. The ACP unique identifier should be used to indicate the corresponding argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.2.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "47" ;
    gsndesign:page "47" .

gsndesign:src-0248
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.2.3 (…) The corresponding argument could be located in a paragraph of accompanying text, a goal in the local argument, or a goal in a separate module."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.2.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "47" ;
    gsndesign:page "47" .

gsndesign:src-0249
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.2.3 (…) Where the corresponding argument is located in a separate module, the module identifier should be shown alongside the ACP identifier delimited with square brackets e.g. ACP1[Confidence]."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.2.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "47" ;
    gsndesign:page "47" .

gsndesign:src-0250
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.1 The presence of an ACP indicates that a separate confidence argument documenting the reasons for having confidence in the relationship or referenced artefact is provided. The nature of confidence arguments is discussed in detail in [8] (Risk, Confidence and Compliance Arguments)."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.3.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "47, 48" ;
    gsndesign:page "47" ,
        "48" .

gsndesign:src-0251
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.1 (…) The separate confidence argument may be documented in the current argument module, or may be contained in a separate confidence argument module, in which case the ACP identifier is extended to include the {module identifier}."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.3.1" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "48" ;
    gsndesign:page "48" .

gsndesign:src-0252
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.2 The {ACP identifier} may be a reference to a goal, a section in a document, or other form of unique reference that can be followed by the reader of the argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.3.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "48" ;
    gsndesign:page "48" .

gsndesign:src-0253
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.3 In Figure 1:5-1, ACP1 is associated with the inferential relationship between G1 and its supporting goals, G2 and G3, via strategy S1. This relationship is indivisible, such that the confidence argument relates to the entirety of support for G1."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ,
        "1:5.3.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "48" ;
    gsndesign:page "48" .

gsndesign:src-0254
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.3 (…) The placement of an ACP on an individual ‘SupportedBy’ relationship below the strategy is ambiguous and should be avoided."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.3.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "48" ;
    gsndesign:page "48" .

gsndesign:src-0255
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.4 An ACP can be placed on the evidential relationship indicated by the ‘SupportedBy’ relationship between a goal and supporting evidence as illustrated in Figure 1:5-2."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ,
        "1:5.3.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "48" ;
    gsndesign:page "48" .

gsndesign:src-0256
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.5 Where a single goal is supported by more than one item of evidence, the ACP applies across all ‘SupportedBy’ relationships in support of the goal and may be illustrated as shown in Figure 1:5-3."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ,
        "1:5.3.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "49" ;
    gsndesign:page "49" .

gsndesign:src-0257
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.5 (…) This representation may also be applied where a goal is supported by multiple goals without a strategy being explicitly represented."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5.3.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "49" ;
    gsndesign:page "49" .

gsndesign:src-0258
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:5.3.6 An ACP may also be associated with an ‘InContextOf’ relationship as illustrated in Figure 1:5-4. This enables a confidence argument to support the contextual relationship."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:5" ,
        "1:5.3.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "49" ;
    gsndesign:page "49" .

gsndesign:src-0259
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """1:6.1.1 A Dialectic process in its simplest form is the investigation of truth. Applied to Assurance Cases, dialectics add strength to arguments by comparing options, testing truth, logically disputing and constructively criticising. The use of a dialectic process provides a framework for creating, challenging and questioning Assurance Cases through the discovery and identification of doubt, which can be depicted and the residual doubt exposed.

1:6.3.8 Figure 1:6-2 depicts a dialectic challenge to a goal. The dialectic challenge within this structure asserts that if the claim presented in Goal CG1 is true then this is sufficient to establish that the claim in Goal G1 in the original structure is in doubt. Thus, a challenge to a target element is documented by identifying a claim that asserts a challenge."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.1.1" ,
        "1:6.3.8" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "50; 53" ;
    gsndesign:page "50" ,
        "53" .

gsndesign:src-0260
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.2.2 GSN defines dialectic uses of the following core elements: • Goal; • Solution"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "50" ;
    gsndesign:page "50" .

gsndesign:src-0261
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.2.3 An additional dialectic specific relationship is provided: • Challenges"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.2.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "50" ;
    gsndesign:page "50" .

gsndesign:src-0262
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.2.4 GSN defines a status that may be assigned to elements and relationships: • Defeated"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.2.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "50" ;
    gsndesign:page "50" .

gsndesign:src-0263
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.2.6 The definitions below apply to all the other ‘forms’ of goals and solutions defined within the GSN Extension Tables throughout the standard for the normative definition i.e. Instantiable (represented within curly brackets), instantiated, undeveloped, public/private, away, as applicable."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.2.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "50" ;
    gsndesign:page "50" .

gsndesign:src-0264
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-1 – Definition
A goal, (core element) can be used in a dialectic context to assert a challenge to part of the argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "51" ;
    gsndesign:page "51" .

gsndesign:src-0265
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-1 – Definition
A solution, (core element) can be used to present a reference to an evidence item that asserts a challenge to part of the argument."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "51" ;
    gsndesign:page "51" .

gsndesign:src-0266
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-1 – Definition
Defeated Element decorator symbol, rendered as a cross (‘X’) superimposed on a GSN element. This indicates that the element is defeated

Table 1:6-2 – Definition 
Defeated Relationship decorator symbol, rendered as a cross (‘X’) superimposed on a GSN relationship. This indicates that the relationship is defeated"""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "51; 52" ;
    gsndesign:page "51" ,
        "52" .

gsndesign:src-0267
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-1 – Definition
Defeated Element decorator symbol, rendered as a cross (‘X’) superimposed on a GSN element. This indicates that the element is defeated"""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "51" ;
    gsndesign:page "51" .

gsndesign:src-0268
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-1 – Definition
The Defeated decorator can be applied to any of the GSN elements."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "51" ;
    gsndesign:page "51" .

gsndesign:src-0269
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.2.7 Table 1:6-2 provides the definition and rendering of relationships for use in the dialectic extension. This declares a relationship between a source element (the entity responsible for making the challenge) and a target element. The arrow points to the target. An additional dialectic decorator is also provided."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.2.7" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "51" ;
    gsndesign:page "51" .

gsndesign:src-0270
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-2 – Definition 
Challenges, rendered as a dashed line with an open arrowhead, allows a Challenge to any GSN entity to be documented."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "51" ;
    gsndesign:page "51" .

gsndesign:src-0271
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-2 – Definition 
Permitted connections are: goal-to-any element, solution-to-any element, goal-to-any relationship, solution-to-any relationship,"""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "51" ;
    gsndesign:page "51" .

gsndesign:src-0272
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-2 – Definition 
Defeated Relationship decorator symbol, rendered as a cross (‘X’) superimposed on a GSN relationship. This indicates that the relationship is defeated"""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "52" ;
    gsndesign:page "52" .

gsndesign:src-0273
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """Table 1:6-2 – Definition 
The Defeated decorator can be applied to any of the GSN relationships."""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "52" ;
    gsndesign:page "52" .

gsndesign:src-0274
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.2 The dialectic extension can be applied to any existing goal structure that complies with the other applicable normative parts of this standard. These may be in progress or deemed to be complete. Any updates that are required to refactor the structure in order to continue the dialectic process are similarly covered by this standard."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "52" ;
    gsndesign:page "52" .

gsndesign:src-0275
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.3 A dialectic challenge, can be levied against any part of a goal structure, referred to here as the target of the challenge."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "52" ;
    gsndesign:page "52" .

gsndesign:src-0276
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.4 A challenge must be levied against the appropriate aspect of the goal structure. For example, it is all too easy to place challenges against a solution (evidence) which is actually valid in its own right, when it is the inference of its use that should be challenged. In such a case, the impact of any resultant defeat on the rest of the goal structure will be unclear and may lead to an invalid goal structure."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "52" ;
    gsndesign:page "52" .

gsndesign:src-0277
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.5 Counter evidence (via a solution) or an evidenced counter argument (via a goal) can be used to support a challenge to any element in a goal structure e.g. goal, solution, strategy, context, assumption, justification including those that are extended by the other extensions to GSN."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.5" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "52" ;
    gsndesign:page "52" .

gsndesign:src-0278
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.6 Figure 1:6-1 depicts a dialectic challenge to a goal that results in defeat. The dialectic challenge within this structure asserts that if the evidence referred to in Solution CSn1 is valid, this is sufficient to establish that the claim in Goal G1 in the original structure is successfully challenged. Thus, a challenge to a target element is documented by identifying the counter evidence that makes this challenge."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "53" ;
    gsndesign:page "53" .

gsndesign:src-0279
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.7 In Figure 1:6-1, the challenge made by the evidence presented in solution Sn1 is valid and the claim presented in goal G1 is defeated. The defeat is depicted by the defeated decorator, which is applied to indicate that goal G1 is no longer valid and so presents a claim left as defeated in the goal structure."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.7" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "53" ;
    gsndesign:page "53" .

gsndesign:src-0280
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.8 Figure 1:6-2 depicts a dialectic challenge to a goal. The dialectic challenge within this structure asserts that if the claim presented in Goal CG1 is true then this is sufficient to establish that the claim in Goal G1 in the original structure is in doubt. Thus, a challenge to a target element is documented by identifying a claim that asserts a challenge."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.8" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "53" ;
    gsndesign:page "53" .

gsndesign:src-0281
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.8 (…) The challenge is complete only once an argument to support the assertion is developed and evidenced and so a counter argument is formed."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.8" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "53" ;
    gsndesign:page "53" .

gsndesign:src-0282
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.9 Counter evidence (via a solution) or an evidenced counter argument (via a goal) can be used to challenge any relationship in a goal structure i.e. SupportedBy, InContextOf, Challenges, including those that are extended by the other extensions to GSN."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.9" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "53" ;
    gsndesign:page "53" .

gsndesign:src-0283
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.10 Figure 1:6-3 depicts a dialectic challenge to a SupportedBy relationship that results in defeat. This is documented by identifying the evidence referred to by Solution CSn1 that asserts this challenge. Thus, a successful challenge to a target relationship is developed by applying counter evidence, similarly to Section 1:6.3.6."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.10" ,
        "1:6.3.6" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "53, 54" ;
    gsndesign:page "53" ,
        "54" .

gsndesign:src-0284
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.11 In Figure 1:6-3, the evidence presented in solution CSn1 defeats the SupportedBy relationship. The defeat is depicted by the defeated decorator, which is applied to indicate that the SupportedBy relationship is no longer valid and so is presented as defeated in the goal structure."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.11" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "54" ;
    gsndesign:page "54" .

gsndesign:src-0285
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.12 As the inference between a goal and its supporting goal is indivisible, it is only possible to challenge the inference relationship in its entirety. A challenge cannot be made directly to multiple SupportedBy relationships, so challenges to this inference require a strategy to be inserted."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.12" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "54" ;
    gsndesign:page "54" .

gsndesign:src-0286
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.12 (…) Figure 1:6-4 depicts a dialectic challenge to a multiple SupportedBy relationship that results in defeat. If in the left-hand goal structure (a) the supporting-goals G2 and G3 are considered not sufficient and suitable to support goal G1 and a challenge to this inference is achieved by inserting strategy S1 below goal G1 and applying the challenge to the new strategy, as in the right-hand goal structure (b). The defeat is depicted by the defeated decorator, which is applied to indicate that the strategy S1 is no longer valid."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.12" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "54" ;
    gsndesign:page "54" .

gsndesign:src-0287
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.13 Figure 1:6-5 depicts a challenge to a Challenges relationship that is documented by identifying a claim represented by goal CG2 that asserts a challenge. Thus, the original challenge can itself be challenged by forming an evidenced counter argument (similarly to Section 1:6.3.8 above). The doubt raised has yet to be resolved."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.13" ,
        "1:6.3.8" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "55" ;
    gsndesign:page "55" .

gsndesign:src-0288
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.14 A challenge may be countered and so may itself be subject to further challenge. A countering challenge may be made to a preceding challenge by challenging: the inference of the challenge (via the Challenges relationship) as in Figure 1:6-5;"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6" ,
        "1:6.3.14" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "55" ;
    gsndesign:page "55" .

gsndesign:src-0289
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.14 (…) counter evidence (via the associated solution); a counter claim (via the goal);"@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.14" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "55" ;
    gsndesign:page "55" .

gsndesign:src-0290
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.3.14 (…) or any part of a supporting evidenced counter argument that supports the counter claim."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.3.14" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "55" ;
    gsndesign:page "55" .

gsndesign:src-0291
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.4.2 In a dialectic context, the goal statement is expressed to make a claim that asserts a challenge to a part of the argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.4.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "56" ;
    gsndesign:page "56" .

gsndesign:src-0292
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.4.3 In a dialectic context, a solution references evidence that challenges part of the argument."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.4.3" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "56" ;
    gsndesign:page "56" .

gsndesign:src-0293
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "1:6.4.4 The goal and solution statements should be clearly expressed such that the crux of the challenge is unequivocally communicated. Thus, the link between the part of the argument that is being challenged (target) and the dialectic element (source) is self-evident."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "1:6.4.4" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "56" ;
    gsndesign:page "56" .

gsndesign:src-0294
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText """2:11.2.2 Dialectic can be used as a prefix:
• ‘Dialectic argument’ - the outcome of using dialectic thinking or process"""@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "2:11.2.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "115" ;
    gsndesign:page "115" .

gsndesign:src-0295
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "Footnote 6: A dialectic element can sometimes be referred to as a ‘defeater’, though it does not necessarily result in defeat."@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "115" ;
    gsndesign:page "115" .

gsndesign:src-0296
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "• ‘Dialectic element’ - the source of challenge being applied6"@en ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "115" ;
    gsndesign:page "115" .

gsndesign:src-0297
    a gsndesign:SourcePassage ;
    prov:specializationOf gsndesign:GSNCommunityStandardV3 ;
    gsndesign:quotedText "2:11.3.1.2 A defeater (goal or solution) can challenge any element in a goal structure, e.g. goal, solution, strategy, context, assumption, justification."@en ;
    gsndesign:clause "2:11.3.1.2" ;
    gsndesign:pageRef "117" ;
    gsndesign:page "117" .


#################################################################
#    Rationales (194)
#    The editorial record: why something was included, excluded or reshaped.
#    Shared, because the same reasoning decided several axioms.
#################################################################

gsndesign:why-0001
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Protege’s automatic declaration of default prefixes for the GSN ontology namespace. The URL is tentative."@en .

gsndesign:why-0002
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Protege’s automatic declaration of prefixes of helper (foundational) ontologies. The use of HTTP instead of HTTPS is due to Protégé."@en .

gsndesign:why-0003
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Imported reusable/helper annotation and datatype properties from foundational ontologies: Schema, SKOS and FOAF."@en .

gsndesign:why-0004
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Base class and object properties for the reification of triples, i.e. statements which allow attaching properties to triples. For example, for some triple ‘A follows B’, we can assert that: A_follows_B is a Statement; A_follows_B has subject A; A_follows_B has predicate follows;  A_follows_B has object B; and A_follows_B is valid."@en .

gsndesign:why-0005
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Protege’s automatic declaration of the universal resource identifier (URI) and membership for the GSN ontology."@en .

gsndesign:why-0006
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Metadata about the ontology creation process."@en .

gsndesign:why-0007
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Important metadata regarding the GSN metamodel."@en .

gsndesign:why-0008
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "These metadata are relevant only for the document (provenance, etc.), and not the current model itself."@en .

gsndesign:why-0009
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Because GSN is a visual language, “renderedAs” provides the geometric description of each element."@en .

gsndesign:why-0010
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Allows each element to be associated with the relevant subset of the normative standard, so that the user can filter out triples of unused extension."@en .

gsndesign:why-0011
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The definitions are duplicates, but both are preserved since they are in the standard."@en .

gsndesign:why-0012
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The meaning of claim in GSN was initially mixed up with claim in the language. After reviewing the standard text, it is clearer that claims only refer to goals."@en .

gsndesign:why-0013
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "It is unclear whether “structured” is a property relevant for evaluation. It is added as a datatype property just in case."@en .

gsndesign:why-0014
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "“Dialectic” is implicitly represented in the “Defeater” concept. For “dialectic argument”, see Part 2."@en .

gsndesign:why-0015
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Because their role in GSN is unclear, and there is an equivalent data property for defining a top goal, “Premise” and “Conclusion” are not defined as subclasses or types of goals in this version."@en .

gsndesign:why-0016
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Argument-Context relationship is undefined in the standard (as opposed to, e.g., Goal-Context), so this part is ignored."@en .

gsndesign:why-0017
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "“Evidence” is represented as an alternative label and not explicitly, due to competing “Artefact Reference” and “Solution” concepts. Unclear how these three concepts interface."@en .

gsndesign:why-0018
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "“Evidence” is represented as an alternative label of an “Artefact” and not as an explicit or separate class, due to competing “Artefact Reference” and “Solution” concepts. If the need arises, it may become a subclass of Artefact."@en .

gsndesign:why-0019
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rule formulated instead."@en .

gsndesign:why-0020
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """“Goal structure” is not represented explicitly, because:
The difference between goal structure, assurance case and argument is unclear;
“Goal structure” does not have any particular properties that cannot be represented in assurance cases and arguments."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0021
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Adding annotations to more advanced domain and range expressions leads to an error, so these statements are added as description."@en .

gsndesign:why-0022
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "SWRL does not support negation or checking for blanks, and can only define class membership"@en .

gsndesign:why-0023
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "SPARQL supports negation, but this option is left for future versions of the ontology, to reduce the number of dependencies."@en .

gsndesign:why-0024
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is an OWL general class axiom, meaning that negation and inversion are supported, but also the inverse of the rule itself is enforced by a reasoner."@en .

gsndesign:why-0025
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Both definitions are preserved because they introduce new information rather than duplicate it."@en .

gsndesign:why-0026
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """“Element” not an explicit concept or alias, because:
GSN elements are essentially a union / superclass of Claims and Artifact References."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0027
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Claim and Artefact Reference can be marked as disjoint – i.e., either an element is a reference or a claim – but this is not explicity stated in the standard. Furthermore, it is not clear how to distinguish between Claim:Context and ArtefactReference:Context. Therefore, this rule is currently inactive."@en .

gsndesign:why-0028
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Context can be a Claim or an Artefact Reference (i.e., a class representing a reference which both Solution and Context fit). However, it is not clear how distinct should a Claim:Context be from ArtefactReference:Context. Clarification needed."@en .

gsndesign:why-0029
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "In addition to defining the “domain” and “range” for the property, OWL restriction axioms were added on “Goal” and “Strategy” for validation purposes."@en .

gsndesign:why-0030
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "In addition to defining of “domain” and “range” for the property, OWL restriction axioms were added on “Goal” and “Strategy”"@en .

gsndesign:why-0031
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Direct prohibited relations are handled by making the property asymmetric. Cycles are handled by checking for the same directed relationship between three different elements."@en .

gsndesign:why-0032
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "SPARQL allows for recursive checking, however, this rule is not active in this version to reduce dependencies in the ontology."@en .

gsndesign:why-0033
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "By defining the properties as irreflexive, it means an element cannot support or contextualize itself. By defining the properties as asymmetric, it means that element A cannot support and also be supported by some element B at the same time."@en .

gsndesign:why-0034
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is an emergent property of the case. These positional aliases (i.e., “parent”, “child”, “supporting” and “sub-“) are not made explicit in this version of the ontology."@en .

gsndesign:why-0035
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Since claims can be valid but untrue, “true” is represented as a separate data property."@en .

gsndesign:why-0036
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Fixed error from v1.2.0 that indicated that `gsn:true(?B, true)`when it should be `false`."@en .

gsndesign:why-0037
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "SPARQL supports negation, but to reduce the number of dependencies, the rule is made inactive for this version."@en .

gsndesign:why-0038
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This rule is deprecated, because the reasoner would evaluate C as true if A and B are true, regardless if more elements (e.g., D, E, ...) which are false support C also."@en .

gsndesign:why-0039
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Beyond a simple propagation, the assertion of truthfulness is implicit and left to the user to validate, since that (i.e., the claim-evidence relation) is the underlying purpose of assurance cases."@en .

gsndesign:why-0040
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Unclear how to implement the concept of indivisibility in the ontology, beyond a simple AND operator."@en .

gsndesign:why-0041
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Given that ‘Context’ cannot be evaluated as true or false, the interpretation of this rule is that an “inContextOf” relation can be evaluated as true or false (e.g., if it is falsely asserted that a claim is true in a given context).
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rule formulated instead."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0042
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "According to the interpretation of this point, a ‘Goal’ must have at least one associated ‘Context’. However, given the contradictory examples provided in other normative and non-normative sections of the standard, it is not clear whether this constraint should apply to all claims, including ‘Context’ class itself."@en .

gsndesign:why-0043
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Unclear how to apply the contradiction or undermining evaluation automatically, and without the additional elements from the Dialectic Extension."@en .

gsndesign:why-0044
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Given that a true ‘Assumption’ is a precondition for a true ‘Goal’, when they are linked, the inverse then becomes a rule."@en .

gsndesign:why-0045
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This lack of propagation means that it follows the “open world” approach – i.e., justification linked to one element is not necessarily linked to another supporting element."@en .

gsndesign:why-0046
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is a property of any user interface used for visualization, and not necessarily of the ontology. Subsequent versions may include a rule for the maximum amount of nodes and edges to be visualized on any given page."@en .

gsndesign:why-0047
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The ‘statement’ datatype property is defined for the text contained in the element. It is datatype instead of annotation, because it is needed for reasoning. The ‘label’ property is reserved for visualization purposes."@en .

gsndesign:why-0048
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Current version of the ontology only provides the grammar rule as a guideline for the user, and does not support automatic checks of correctness. Checks could later be added by explicating each noun- and verb-phrase, and verifying that the element has the right combination."@en .

gsndesign:why-0049
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "“Artefact Reference” is not a term used by the standard, but the concept is: 1:2.3.4 states that solutions “are simply references to evidence items”, and 1:2.3.5 that a context statement may be “a reference to an artefact of some kind”. Solution and Context are therefore both references to an artefact, and any rule that holds for both belongs on a common superclass. That superclass is introduced here as “Artefact Reference”, with refersTo linking the reference to the artefact itself."@en .

gsndesign:why-0050
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """In order to be able to execute rules (e.g., if X supportedBy Y is true, do Z) or make assertions (e.g., X supportedBy Y is invalid) about specific relationships, we need to reify that triple into a Relationship class. This is an ontology-specific assertion.
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0051
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Object properties are intentionally asserted as classes (“punning”), to allow connection to Relationships via rdf:predicate.
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0052
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Triples containing “supportedBy” and “inContextOf” are automatically reified. 
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rules are defined instead."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0053
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is addressed under section 1:3.5."@en .

gsndesign:why-0054
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Rendering added to min and max cardinality for the purpose of visualization."@en .

gsndesign:why-0055
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "SHACL constraints can add default values and advanced restrictions. However, this constraint is not active, in order to reduce the number of dependencies in this ontology version."@en .

gsndesign:why-0056
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This remains implicit, since there is already a rule indicating that default minCardinality is 1, and minCardinality can be a non-negative integer."@en .

gsndesign:why-0057
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "See minCardinality and maxCardinality datatype properties."@en .

gsndesign:why-0058
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already allowed in the ontology."@en .

gsndesign:why-0059
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "See SHACL rule under Table 1:3-1"@en .

gsndesign:why-0060
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Location of the decorator is not indicated in this version. Future versions of the ontology can make visual rules explicit."@en .

gsndesign:why-0061
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is a visualization rule, and thus not included in the ontology."@en .

gsndesign:why-0062
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is a visualization rule, and thus not included in the ontology. Nothing in the ontology restricts overlaying. Future versions of the ontology can make visual rules explicit."@en .

gsndesign:why-0063
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "In order to enforce the weaker constraint (i.e., some textual description), the description annotation property is also defined as a datatype property. Future version can be revised to enforce the stronger constraint (i.e., description must contain components under subsections in 1:3.4.1)."@en .

gsndesign:why-0064
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is the stronger constraint, which is not enforced in this version."@en .

gsndesign:why-0065
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already covered with the “label” annotation property."@en .

gsndesign:why-0066
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already covered with the “altLabel” annotation property."@en .

gsndesign:why-0067
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This section can actually be a collection of descriptions of all “participating” elements. Next version of ontology can include “participatesIn” as an object property, a “description” annotation property for pattern elements, and a rule collating descriptions."@en .

gsndesign:why-0068
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Next version of the standard could address applicability by allowing an “inContextOf” relation between a pattern and context."@en .

gsndesign:why-0069
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Next version of the standard could address consequences by allowing an “inContextOf” relation between a pattern and assumption."@en .

gsndesign:why-0070
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Currently, there is nothing in the standard that would allow"@en .

gsndesign:why-0071
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Both the Template Argument and an element within the Template Argument are invalid if they contain the undeveloped decorator."@en .

gsndesign:why-0072
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "It is not clear how to implement this rule."@en .

gsndesign:why-0073
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Although IDR would conceptually fit as a subclass of “Artefact Reference”, this sentence indicates that it should not be the case."@en .

gsndesign:why-0074
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Core GSN, not Modular Extension. Containment is already a core notion: an argument may contain other arguments, and an assurance case may contain arguments and artefacts. The Modular Extension only widens the domain and range of ‘contains’ to include modules; it does not introduce the property. The design document originally recorded “Modular Extension”, which was the narrower reading."@en .

gsndesign:why-0075
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Away is defined as a datatype property."@en .

gsndesign:why-0076
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Because away is a property of an element supporting or contextualizing another element, only the former should have the."@en .

gsndesign:why-0077
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The open world assumption allows same identifiers, so we define a rule when it is not allowed (i.e., in the same module)."@en .

gsndesign:why-0078
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """This “renderedAs” property is inserted through a rule that overwrites the class-level “renderedAs” assertion. Next ontology version can include data needed for visualizing directly.
EDIT 21-02-25: SWRL cannot assert annotations; rule disabled unless renderedAs should be redefined as a datatype property."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0079
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """The shape description is interpreted, since the standard only refers to “as shown left”. This “renderedAs” property is inserted through a rule that overwrites the class-level “renderedAs” assertion. Next ontology version can include data needed for visualizing directly.
EDIT 21-02-25: SWRL cannot assert annotations; rule disabled unless renderedAs should be redefined as a datatype property."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0080
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Adding annotations to more advanced domain and range expressions leads to an error, so these statements are added as description. For the definition, the statement is formulated with a reference to an element."@en .

gsndesign:why-0081
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "No assertion is needed, since nothing changes except the rendering for the away element."@en .

gsndesign:why-0082
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already implicit in the relation. No further restriction is made (conditional transitivity, e.g., if X supportedBy Y and Y contains Z, then X supportedBy Z)."@en .

gsndesign:why-0083
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rules are defined instead."@en .

gsndesign:why-0084
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Although it can be considered a subclass of a module, contract is defined as a boolean datatype property."@en .

gsndesign:why-0085
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "See comment on the statement for module references."@en .

gsndesign:why-0086
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This rule is already covered by preventing any kind of module references (incl. where contract is true) being used in contract modules."@en .

gsndesign:why-0087
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is a visualization rule, and thus not included in this version."@en .

gsndesign:why-0088
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Although this can be represented as a “supportedBy” link between a goal and a contract module that is undisclosed, the decorator is explicitly applied to a goal."@en .

gsndesign:why-0089
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Selective display in argument and architecture views seems to be for visualization purposes, and not directly ontology-relevant."@en .

gsndesign:why-0090
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "“Mutually exclusive” is represented with propertyDisjointWith in OWL."@en .

gsndesign:why-0091
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "No additional constraints seem to be specified here. Development of away elements in the module under view is already constrained."@en .

gsndesign:why-0092
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The “away_x-to-away_x“ clause contradicts the rule that no away element should be developed in the module under view. This constraint would need clarification or a condition to ignore this when visualizing contract modules."@en .

gsndesign:why-0093
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Added the possibility for a strategy or a goal to be in the context of a module.

In addition to defining of “domain” and “range” for the property, OWL restriction axioms were added on “Goal” and “Strategy”"""@en .

gsndesign:why-0094
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "No additional constraints are specified here, so nothing needs to be represented."@en .

gsndesign:why-0095
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Decomposing an away goal in a contract module does not make it automatically valid, because there may be other reasons the goal is invalid. Instead, the module in which it is decomposed is marked as a contract."@en .

gsndesign:why-0096
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already covered by a regular supportedBy property."@en .

gsndesign:why-0097
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "There is no constraint preventing this, so no change to the ontology is needed."@en .

gsndesign:why-0098
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Although substitution is not mentioned elsewhere, it is defined as a separate property to allow an away goal to stand for a justification, while still being consistent with the Core GSN (i.e., inContextOf range only Context, Assumption, Justification)."@en .

gsndesign:why-0099
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "In order to support further reasoning (i.e., abstraction of contextual relations between modules), a justification that is substituted must be in the same module as the goal; otherwise it is invalid."@en .

gsndesign:why-0100
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Already added as a viewType earlier."@en .

gsndesign:why-0101
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "There is no need to specify further restrictions here, because the architecture view is just an abstraction of relations between elements contained by two or more modules. More details below."@en .

gsndesign:why-0102
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Modules already have renderedAs information, and exclusion of a line is purely a visualization choice."@en .

gsndesign:why-0103
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Existence of module identifiers is enforced,"@en .

gsndesign:why-0104
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Possibility of description is added, but not enforced."@en .

gsndesign:why-0105
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Identifiers are enforced. The rest is purely a visualization choice."@en .

gsndesign:why-0106
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Possibility of description is there."@en .

gsndesign:why-0107
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Since this is purely a choice of visualization, and the building blocks already exist, nothing needs to be modified in the ontology itself."@en .

gsndesign:why-0108
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Since this is an abstraction of relations between the elements contained in two separate modules, and nothing from earlier statements restricts using existing relations for modules, there is no need to define separate object properties."@en .

gsndesign:why-0109
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Relations between elements in separate modules are abstracted as relations between the modules themselves, for the purpose of the architecture view."@en .

gsndesign:why-0110
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Since entire modules can be added as support to a goal, the supportedBy property between a goal and a module is considered as a relation between the latter and the module containing the goal."@en .

gsndesign:why-0111
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already covered in the rules for the previous statement (directly above)."@en .

gsndesign:why-0112
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Ontologies support multiple and bidirectional relations, including for visualization purposes within Protege, so “composite” and “bidirectionality” are purely stylistic choices."@en .

gsndesign:why-0113
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already addressed above. In future versions, it would be possible to add a quantifier showing how many particular relations are between modules."@en .

gsndesign:why-0114
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "No further restrictions are specified."@en .

gsndesign:why-0115
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This rule is specified only for the simplest case of circularity. Cyclic graphs with more nodes will require more advanced solutions."@en .

gsndesign:why-0116
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is added as a note for the user."@en .

gsndesign:why-0117
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The use of module interface connectors is purely a choice of visualization aid. Given that building blocks for such visualization exist, but it is not mandatory, there is no need to specify anything additional in the ontology."@en .

gsndesign:why-0118
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The configuration information is not specified elsewhere. Therefore, the default assumption is that it should be included as part of the description."@en .

gsndesign:why-0119
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The indicated elements of a visualization already exist as building blocks. Visualization choices should be handled outside the ontology. However, future versions of the ontology can encode visualization rules if necessary."@en .

gsndesign:why-0120
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is treated as a general rule regardless of visualization choices."@en .

gsndesign:why-0121
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "These requirements regarding uniqueness of identifiers are handled in earlier rules."@en .

gsndesign:why-0122
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This statement is provided as a note for the user, because it provides details that are relevant for creators of assurance cases, but otherwise does not have clear rules for checking validity."@en .

gsndesign:why-0123
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This statement does not provide enough information regarding the relation between a contract module and a justification."@en .

gsndesign:why-0124
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Currently not clear how to handle “interfaces that have been made visible to it”. Clarification is needed."@en .

gsndesign:why-0125
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Currently not clear how to handle “[contained modules] are private ot the containing contract module”. Clarification is needed."@en .

gsndesign:why-0126
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """These OWL restrictions ensure that, for some Relationship with Confidence, there exists at least one associated argument and at least one assurance claim point value, and that only confidence arguments can be associated with it.

Because ACPs are only usable on core GSN relation types, additional class and restriction are added to exclude extensions (i.e., Dialectic Extension). 
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules.

Added constraint to confirm that a RelationshipWithConfidence must have ACP and an associated argument, and that the associated argument should be a confidence argument. The referenced source is not a direct part of the standard, and is therefore treated as non-normative for this ontology version. Future versions can include assertions from the source."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0127
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """These OWL restrictions ensure that, for some Relationship with Confidence, there exists at least one associated argument and at least one assurance claim point value, and that only confidence arguments can be associated with it.

Added constraint to confirm that a RelationshipWithConfidence must have ACP and an associated argument, and that the associated argument should be a confidence argument. The referenced source is not a direct part of the standard, and is therefore treated as non-normative for this ontology version. Future versions can include assertions from the source."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0128
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Because ACPs are only usable on core GSN relation types, additional class and restriction are added to exclude extensions (i.e., Dialectic Extension). 
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0129
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "To ensure that Artefact References with ACPs have identifiers, we first have to enforce that they’re associated with a RelationshipWithConfidence."@en .

gsndesign:why-0130
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "There is no restriction that would not allow this."@en .

gsndesign:why-0131
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Because a separate confidence argument is expected, the associatedWith link between RelationshipWithConfidence and Argument is expected."@en .

gsndesign:why-0132
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The indicated SWRL constraint is inactive, because it is a visualization-relevant rule. If future ontology versions address visualizations, they can include this constraint."@en .

gsndesign:why-0133
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Added constraint to confirm that a RelationshipWithConfidence must have ACP and an associated argument, and that the associated argument should be a confidence argument. The referenced source is not a direct part of the standard, and is therefore treated as non-normative for this ontology version. Future versions can include assertions from the source."@en .

gsndesign:why-0134
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "See point under 1:5.2.3 regarding visualization-relevant rules."@en .

gsndesign:why-0135
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This was addressed earlier using a RelationshipWithConfidence class, and an associatedWith relation with an Argument class."@en .

gsndesign:why-0136
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """If for a given Goal, an ACP (and relevant things) exists for a relation with one Strategy, then all Goals supporting that Strategy should have the same.
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rules are defined instead."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0137
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """If for a given Strategy, an ACP (and relevant things) exists for a relation with one or more Goals, then the Goal supported by the Strategy should have the same.
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rules are defined instead."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0138
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already allowed in the Relationship class, and there are no restrictions preventing this."@en .

gsndesign:why-0139
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """If for a given Goal, an ACP (and relevant things) exists for a relation with one Solution, then all other Solutions supporting that Goal should have the same.
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rules are defined instead."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0140
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """If for a given Goal, an ACP (and relevant things) exists for a relation with one Goal, then all other Goals supporting that Goal should have the same.
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rules are defined instead."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0141
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This is already covered above."@en .

gsndesign:why-0142
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "InDoubt is not the same as invalid, therefore it is a new datatype property."@en .

gsndesign:why-0143
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "There are no restrictions on elements with true property values for the indicated properties, so this should apply by default."@en .

gsndesign:why-0144
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Direction of the arrow is determined automatically."@en .

gsndesign:why-0145
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "GSN elements are either Claims or Artefact References."@en .

gsndesign:why-0146
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Restrictions are added to ensure only permitted connections are added. This can be simplified in the next version by putting this restriction on the Defeater class, and then requiring first that all Defeaters (i.e., Goals or Solutions that challenge other elements) are categorized accordingly."@en .

gsndesign:why-0147
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Target is not included, because it is covered by rdf:object in a Relationship  where rdf:predicate is “challenges”"@en .

gsndesign:why-0148
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Because there are no rules that dictate how to ensure the “appropriate” element is challenged, this instruction is added as a note for the user."@en .

gsndesign:why-0149
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This part is interpreted as covering the Defeater (Dialectic Extension, as subclass of Claim) and RelationshipWithConfidence (Confidence Argument Extension, as subclass of Relationship), so as subclasses, they inherit their link to the challenges object property. Modules and Patterns are not GSN elements as such, and thus not included."@en .

gsndesign:why-0150
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Although extensions are meant to be included, it is not clear how a challenge would interact with the Modular Extension (e.g., substituted by), Argument Pattern Extension (e.g., instantiation of) or Confidence Argument Extension (e.g., associated with).
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0151
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Object properties are intentionally asserted as classes (“punning”), to allow connection to Relationships via rdf:predicate."@en .

gsndesign:why-0152
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Triples containing “challenges” are automatically reified.
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rules are defined instead."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0153
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This rule is already represented in 1:6.3.6."@en .

gsndesign:why-0154
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Instead of just evaluating when a defeater erroneously covers only a part of a composite relationship, it is also possible to: 
1. partially rectify this by adding a strategy and the relevant properties with respect to other elements, and then 
2. indicate where the user should make remaining changes.
EDIT 21-02-25: Punning disabled because of conflict with SWRL rules. New rules are defined instead."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0155
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This rule is already represented in 1:6.3.6. There is no indication that the defeated status propagates across the upstream and downstream “supportedBy” goals."@en .

gsndesign:why-0156
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Given the open world assumption of OWL, and no rules which constrain this, there is no need to represent the \"can be\" axioms."@en .

gsndesign:why-0157
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Because it is difficult to automatically ensure that the Defeater’s statement is relevant to the statement of its target, this note is left for the author."@en .

gsndesign:why-0158
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "“Defeater” is added as a concept, although it is only used as an additional class."@en .

gsndesign:why-0159
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "“Dialectic Element” is an alternative label, since “defeater” is a clearer term with similar semantics in other standards (e.g., CAE). If needed, “dialectic” can be redefined as a data property."@en .

gsndesign:why-0160
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """Defeater is an “emergent class” of a goal or solution that challenges another element, and elements should be classified as such when they fulfill these conditions.

Stated as rdfs:subClassOf rather than owl:equivalentClass. An equivalence would be a biconditional, making every Goal and Solution a Defeater, which would render the “inferred, not asserted” note and the challenges-implies-Defeater rule inert. The subclass axiom states the necessary condition that was intended."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0161
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Defeater is an “emergent class” of a goal or solution that challenges another element, and elements should be classified as such when they fulfill these conditions."@en .

gsndesign:why-0162
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Currently, there is no solution for excluding individuals from being members of the Defeater class when they are no longer challenging another element."@en .

gsndesign:why-0163
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This note is added to deter users from adding standalone defeaters (without referring to a goal or a solution), or interferring with the SWRL rules."@en .

gsndesign:why-0164
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "gsn:Relationship is a reification (rdfs:subClassOf rdf:Statement) used to attach decorators and confidence to a GSN link."@en .

gsndesign:why-0165
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The ontology states the subject of a relationship as Defeater or Goal or Strategy. gsn:Defeater is the inferred union of gsn:Goal and gsn:Solution, so it is expanded here to avoid the shape depending on an asserted gsn:Defeater type."@en .

gsndesign:why-0166
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The declared rdfs:range of gsn:contains (Argument, Artefact, Module) is narrower than the class-level owl:allValuesFrom axioms that use it (e.g. gsn:Argument contains only gsn:GSNElement, gsn:Catalogue contains only gsn:Pattern). The range is therefore validated per containing class rather than globally. gsn:Catalogue is added to the domain here for the same reason."@en .

gsndesign:why-0167
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "gsn:Module is added to the declared domain (Goal, Strategy) because gsn:Module carries an owl:allValuesFrom restriction on gsn:inContextOf."@en .

gsndesign:why-0168
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "gsn:Module is added to the declared domain (Goal, Strategy) because gsn:Module carries an owl:allValuesFrom restriction on gsn:supportedBy."@en .

gsndesign:why-0169
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "An absent statement is incompleteness and is reported per class as a warning; two competing statements on one element is a defect. The GSN Community Standard v3 requires a single statement per element, although the ontology does not declare gsn:statement functional."@en .

gsndesign:why-0170
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """From the general class axiom: ( Goal and not (inverse gsn:supportedBy some Goal) ) EquivalentTo ( gsn:top value true ).

Encodes the general class axiom ( Goal and not (inverse gsn:supportedBy some Goal) ) EquivalentTo ( gsn:top value true ). The two branches of the sh:or read: either the goal is not declared to be a top goal, or nothing in the structure is supported by it."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0171
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The converse direction of the general class axiom: a gsn:Goal that does not support any other gsn:Goal is a top goal and should be asserted as such."@en .

gsndesign:why-0172
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Not an explicit OWL axiom, but implied by GSN: a goal structure is a directed acyclic graph rooted in one or more top goals."@en .

gsndesign:why-0173
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "A pattern catalogue may be created to collate a series of patterns; where such a catalogue is created the structure and format of the definition should be consistent and each pattern’s definition should have a unique {pattern identifier}."@en .

gsndesign:why-0174
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This symbol indicates that the GSN argument below the attached element is to be instantiated as a template argument. It provides a reference to the information used to instantiate the template argument."@en .

gsndesign:why-0175
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "A template argument is a special case of a pattern argument. It uses the core GSN and argument pattern extension to construct an argument structure which requires no further development."@en .

gsndesign:why-0176
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "GSN convention rather than an explicit OWL axiom: the undeveloped decorator indicates that a line of argument has NOT been developed, so the element should carry no gsn:supportedBy links."@en .

gsndesign:why-0177
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """The ten pattern documentation sections all declare rdfs:domain gsn:Pattern.

The ten pattern documentation sections of the GSN Community Standard v3 are modelled as annotation properties with rdfs:domain gsn:Pattern."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0178
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Goal structures can be partitioned into separate, but interrelated, modules. This can allow the division of an overall goal structure into separate goal structures focusing on particular aspects of the overall argument. A module may contain one or more arguments and may contain other modules."@en .

gsndesign:why-0179
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The argument view depicts the argument inside an individual module."@en .

gsndesign:why-0180
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "By making the relationship to the away goal the author is asserting not only the inference of support for the parent goal, but also that the context in which the away goal is declared is consistent with the context and assumptions in scope for the parent goal."@en .

gsndesign:why-0181
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "There may be occasions when a goal or strategy requires fuller justification than can be provided within the confines of a GSN justification element. In such cases, an away goal can be substituted for the justification."@en .

gsndesign:why-0182
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "An away element reference repeats a claim or evidence presented in another argument module."@en .

gsndesign:why-0183
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This indicates that the element is publicly visible in one or more interfaces of the module and can be referenced as an away element."@en .

gsndesign:why-0184
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText """This axiom is asserted on gsn:Argument but annotated as part of the Confidence Argument Extension.

Encodes the class axiom owl:unionOf ( (contains some ArtefactReference) and (contains some Goal) , statement some xsd:string )."""@en .

gsndesign:why-0185
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "An Assurance Claim Point (ACP) can be used in GSN to indicate that a confidence argument is associated with an assertion in a risk argument."@en .

gsndesign:why-0186
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Derived from SWRL rule S40 and from the class axioms on gsn:RelationshipWithConfidence: marking an assurance claim point is what associates a confidence argument with an assertion in a risk argument."@en .

gsndesign:why-0187
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The source of challenge being applied; can be directed at any part of an argument. Membership of gsn:Defeater is only meant to be inferred, not asserted — this shape checks that any asserted membership at least agrees with the equivalent class ( gsn:Goal or gsn:Solution )."@en .

gsndesign:why-0188
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Allows a Challenge to any GSN entity to be documented. A goal, (core element) can be used in a dialectic context to assert a challenge to part of the argument. A solution, (core element) can be used to present a reference to an evidence item that asserts a challenge to part of the argument."@en .

gsndesign:why-0189
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The declared rdfs:range is gsn:GSNElement only, but gsn:Goal and gsn:Solution both carry an owl:allValuesFrom restriction admitting gsn:Relationship as well, and the standard states that a challenge can be directed at any part of an argument. gsn:Relationship is therefore admitted here."@en .

gsndesign:why-0190
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "GSN convention rather than an explicit OWL axiom: the link between the part of the argument that is being challenged (target) and the dialectic element (source) must be self-evident, so an element cannot challenge itself."@en .

gsndesign:why-0191
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "This decorator symbol indicates that the element is defeated. The Defeated decorator can be applied to any of the GSN elements."@en .

gsndesign:why-0192
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The dialectic challenge within this structure asserts that if the claim presented in Goal CG1 is true then this is sufficient to establish that the claim in Goal G1 in the original structure is in doubt."@en .

gsndesign:why-0193
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "A defeated element has been successfully challenged, so it cannot simultaneously be asserted as true."@en .

gsndesign:why-0194
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Complements, rather than replaces, the SWRL rules of the Dialectic Extension: if the claim presented in the challenging goal is true then this is sufficient to establish that the claim in the challenged element is in doubt. Run the reasoner if the assertion is expected to be inferred."@en .

gsndesign:why-0200
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The instantiation data reference is described in the standard as indicating that the GSN argument below the attached element is to be instantiated as a template argument. The thing attached to is an element of the argument, so the range admits a GSN element as well as a template. It is a union rather than a replacement because a template is a pattern, and a pattern falls outside gsn:GSNElement; naming only the element class would have made attachment to a whole template illegal."@en .
gsndesign:why-0201
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "An assurance case is a body of information presented to establish a claim, and the claim it establishes is the case's own. The statement is how OntoGSN records the claim text of anything that makes one, and a case makes one. gsn:inDoubt already admitted the class for the same kind of property."@en .
gsndesign:why-0202
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "In the Argument Pattern Extension the artefact that is developed, finalised and released for reuse is the pattern, and a template argument is a special case of a pattern argument which requires no further development. Finality and publication are properties of that reusable artefact, not only of an argument instantiated from it. A template is a pattern, so the union covers templates without naming them."@en .
gsndesign:why-0203
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Validity is a decorator in the same family as defeat, and the standard says of that family that it can be applied to any of the GSN elements and, separately, to any of the GSN relationships. What can be defeated can be invalid. An argument and a pattern are equally things whose soundness a reader judges, so they carry the flag too. gsn:Relationship is admitted by widening the domain rather than by subclassing gsn:GSNElement, which would have forced an identifier onto every reified relationship."@en .
gsndesign:why-0204
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "A pattern is an argument structure, and an argument structure holds the elements it is built from. A template argument in particular uses the core GSN and argument pattern extension to construct an argument structure, so the elements of that structure are inside the template. An argument likewise holds the pattern it is instantiating while the instantiation is in progress."@en .
gsndesign:why-0205
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The Dialectic Extension provides a relationship of its own: an additional dialectic specific relationship is provided, Challenges. A challenge is therefore a fourth kind of relationship alongside the contextual, evidential and inferential relationships of Core GSN, and the enumeration of relationship types has to name it for a challenge to be typed at all."@en .
gsndesign:why-0206
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "A top goal presents the pinnacle claim in an argument, and it is top in terms of the argument hierarchy rather than necessarily its physical layout. Position in the hierarchy is what decides it, and a goal reached through a strategy sits below the claim that strategy decomposes, so it is not the pinnacle claim. The axiom is stated as an equivalence in both directions deliberately: asserting that a goal is top is a claim about the hierarchy, and where the hierarchy contradicts it the constraint reports a violation."@en .
gsndesign:why-0207
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "A challenge may be directed at any part of an argument, and gsn:challenges is defined as allowing a challenge to any GSN entity to be documented. The reified form of a relationship therefore has to admit a relationship as its object, and the decorators that record the outcome of a challenge have to reach a relationship as well as an element."@en .
gsndesign:why-0208
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Goal structures can be partitioned into separate but interrelated modules, and the standard says a module may contain one or more arguments and may contain other modules. Containment by a module is read strictly as that: the elements of the partition are reached through the argument, not addressed directly. gsn:inModule names the result of that traversal so that the rules of the Modular Extension can state module membership in one atom, and so that membership carries up through nested modules. It is inferred and never authored. The strict reading also settles a question the axiom leaves implicit: gsn:contains is not transitive, because transitivity would entail that a module directly contains the elements of its argument and would make every populated module inconsistent with the restriction."@en .
gsndesign:why-0209
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "An assurance claim point may be placed on any relationship in the argument, including a goal-to-goal inference, so the reified confidence relationship admits a goal as its object. Rule S40 reifies a claim point as soon as an element is flagged as one and nothing in its premises binds the confidence argument, so the relationship it creates is a stub for a person to complete; the association is therefore expected rather than required, and its absence is reported as a warning."@en .

gsndesign:why-0211
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Every class-level restriction on gsn:contains writes values the declared axioms did not admit, on both sides. On the object side, gsn:Argument is constrained to contain only a GSN element or a pattern and is required to contain a goal and an artefact reference, and gsn:Catalogue contains only patterns; none of those classes was in the range. On the subject side, gsn:Catalogue carries a contains restriction but was absent from the domain. Domain and range are generative rather than restrictive, so the narrower axioms did not reject anything — they retyped it: a goal held by an argument was inferred to be an argument, an artefact or a module, and a catalogue holding a pattern was inferred to be one of the four classes then in the domain. Both are widened to the union the ontology's own restrictions already write. gsn:Module is dropped from the range as redundant, being a subclass of gsn:GSNElement; gsn:Pattern is added because it is not."@en .

gsndesign:why-0212
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "There is still no global range shape for gsn:contains, and the comment records why. The owl:allValuesFrom axioms on the containers are each narrower than the declared range and narrower in different directions: an argument may hold a GSN element or a pattern but not an artefact, an assurance case may hold an argument or an artefact but neither a goal nor a pattern, and a catalogue may hold only patterns. What a container may hold is a fact about the container, so it is validated per containing class. The permitted subjects are unchanged from the earlier version of this shape; only the comment is, because the domain mismatch it used to describe was fixed in the ontology."@en .

gsndesign:why-0210
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Table 1:3-3 defines an instantiation data reference as something that “provides a reference to the information used to instantiate the template argument”. It is therefore a reference to an artefact in exactly the sense 1:2.3.4 and 1:2.3.5 give the term for solutions and context, and refersTo is the property the ontology already uses to link a reference to the thing referenced. Naming only gsn:ArtefactReference in the domain left the standard's own description of the symbol unexpressible: an instantiation data reference could be attached to a template but could not say what data it drew on. Subsuming it under gsn:ArtefactReference is not the alternative, because that class is a subclass of gsn:GSNElement and would oblige every instantiation data reference to carry a schema:identifier and to count as an element of the notation; the standard renders it as an annotation on the structure, not as a node in it. Widening the domain to the union asserts only what the standard says and leaves the class hierarchy alone."@en .


gsndesign:why-0220
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "A solution is a reference to an evidence item, so an instantiated solution is expected to point at an artefact. A solution that still carries a placeholder statement, such as \"<evidence>\", has no evidence to point at: its evidence exists only after instantiation. Requiring the reference of every solution therefore warned on every uninstantiated solution in a pattern and on every uninstantiated copy in an instantiated argument. The requirement is split in two. gsnsh:SolutionShape keeps the class check, so a reference to something that is not an artefact is still reported whether the solution is instantiated or not. gsnsh:SolutionEvidenceShape requires the reference only of a solution that is not flagged gsn:uninstantiated true. The exemption rests on the flag of the solution, not on membership in a gsn:Pattern, because an instantiated argument also holds uninstantiated copies, and a pattern can name a fixed evidence item."@en .

