gsndesign:why-0215
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The GSN Community Standard names this term but nowhere defines it: the passage cited on the decision uses the term, or describes the symbol that renders it, without saying what it means. The definition is therefore this ontology's wording and not the standard's, and is prefixed [OntoGSN] in the ontology so that a reader of the Turtle alone can tell the two apart without consulting this record. It was drafted from the cited passage rather than composed freely, so that it still rests on something the standard says. Written because the absence of a definition on these terms was the ontology's largest usability gap and the only substantive finding OOPS! reports against it (pitfall P08, missing annotations); leaving them undefined kept the ontology close to the standard at the cost of leaving its own additions unexplained, which is not a trade the contribution needs to make."@en .

gsndesign:why-0216
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The reified relationship is this ontology's device and not the standard's. GSN draws a link as a line between two symbols and gives it no identity of its own, so there is no passage on which a definition could rest. It exists here because a GSN link carries decorators - multiplicity, optionality, an assurance claim point - and a decorated line cannot be modelled as a bare triple. The definition is therefore wholly this ontology's, is prefixed [OntoGSN], and is the one definition among the twenty-four that cites no source at all."@en .

gsndesign:why-0217
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The standard defines this term outright, so the definition is its own sentence rather than a paraphrase, and carries no [OntoGSN] prefix. Two things were elided: the framing that introduces the sentence as the definition of a decorator, and any clause saying where the symbol is drawn, which gsn:renderedAs already carries on the same term. A cross-reference into another section of the standard was dropped for the same reason - it carries no meaning outside the document it points into."@en .


#################################################################
#    Added by prov_add.py (7 decisions)
#    Drafts. Each still needs a source passage and a rationale written by hand.
#################################################################

gsndesign:why-0218
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "Seven annotation properties imported so that the ontology's own header can state what the FAIR assessors look for and found missing: a preferred namespace URI (vann), a DOI and a publication status (bibo), a logo and the catalogue the ontology is deposited in (schema), and issue and modification dates (dcterms). FOOPS! scored the ontology 75% - the version IRI, the namespace URI, the DOI, the logo, the status, the issued date and a registry entry were the seven things it could not find, and each is a fact about the release that was true but nowhere written down. dcterms:issued and dcterms:modified replace dc:issued and dc:modified, which were never Dublin Core Elements 1.1 terms at all: DCMI defines issued and modified only in DCMI Metadata Terms, so the two properties the ontology had been using resolved to nothing and no tool read them. The DOI is Zenodo's concept DOI rather than a version DOI, so that it keeps pointing at the newest deposit rather than pinning the first; schema:includedInDataCatalog names that same Zenodo deposit, which is what makes the claim true - the check also passes on a Linked Open Vocabularies entry, which the ontology does not yet have."@en .

gsndesign:why-0219
    a gsndesign:Rationale ;
    gsndesign:rationaleText "The standard introduces the argument view as the view that depicts the argument inside an individual module (1:4.2.1). The ontology already carried gsn:View, with gsn:viewType to tell an argument view from an architecture view, but no property related a view to the module whose argument it shows, so a view could not be placed in the case it belongs to. gsn:depicts names that relation, and its domain and range are the two nouns of the sentence: the view depicts, and a module is what it depicts. The domain is the whole gsn:View class rather than only views typed as argument views, because the ontology does not subclass views by type and the sentence is read as the view's purpose, not as a restriction on which views have one. The property is authored, never inferred. The two SHACL shapes state the same domain and range for a validator, so that a case which depicts something other than a module, or depicts from something other than a view, is reported rather than retyped."@en .


